[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} {:answer/id #uuid "9872acd7-7eb7-50ae-9b3c-851760c20bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(1-2t)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331311} {:answer/id #uuid "99ac4658-4973-5057-bf1a-ac90579a65ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331325} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4c8a27-8293-5412-8e68-c58c6bc8b6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(2t-1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331741} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2c6554-4cb6-5fba-be9f-c05762bf19fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{-4t}(t+1)}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f191b13-825b-5416-8838-96dcdfe09985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330218} :answer-field/id #uuid "209fa9dc-670a-542f-8478-5c1255d77e7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daa33906-2d77-5e9e-b519-e39e6466e412" :question/math-academy-id "q-50448" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y={e}^{-4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-1)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{-2{e}^{-4t}}{t})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{{t}^{2}})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t{e}^{-4t}+2{e}^{-4t}}{2{t}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-4t}(4t+1)}{{t}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332824 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7434f6cd-5abd-5918-983c-90f901173537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467650} {:answer/id #uuid "390198b7-c54f-5cd9-9c6c-20eb14f25176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "390198b7-c54f-5cd9-9c6c-20eb14f25176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467651} :answer-field/id #uuid "9223a194-f14e-5bca-a4c3-a9be1301213f" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8c9699-34bd-5a5b-b1d1-7fc0cd00c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467652} {:answer/id #uuid "32c64784-f8cf-5e6a-8345-7313e29025cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8c9699-34bd-5a5b-b1d1-7fc0cd00c967" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467652} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec5739d-20d1-54f9-9ab8-3fc8589202be" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468547} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0e3c250-5a95-5d66-9b29-c6400da6d824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467654} {:answer/id #uuid "c5386d8a-3465-59f6-895b-f357aefa1a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5386d8a-3465-59f6-895b-f357aefa1a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467655} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f75b307-815b-51f7-b01e-ead1c5a03e0b" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29977f9f-fbdd-580d-914b-4f20cb78cbe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-50506" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ converge."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf2a9bb-f82a-58f8-a20c-b05ede19e65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330924} {:answer/id #uuid "bd396760-757f-5cc3-ae92-a73a9c032976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331619} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ea34c-687a-52c0-b80d-c348d15417e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331898} {:answer/id #uuid "ee599243-e5a7-5411-bebf-da12a4e46cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332043} {:answer/id #uuid "f66fc1fd-c8f4-5efe-88c2-728e48b39059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0ea34c-687a-52c0-b80d-c348d15417e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331898} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b79c0ce-dcc8-507c-809f-b44d117a88c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "368258e5-5111-5755-bf64-912ecff12c3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-50607" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }a{r}^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{kn}\\cdot {3}^{-2}}{{9}^{n-1}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n-1}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{3}^{kn}}{{9}^{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r=\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r|<1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{k}}{9} & <1 \\\\ {3}^{k} & <9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\frac{{3}^{k}}{9}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation ${3}^{k}=9$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since ${3}^{k}$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to ${3}^{k}<9$ must be $k<2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3374134f-1ab4-5262-8e8c-f1702edc87a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330403} {:answer/id #uuid "621ec133-af6e-5549-9575-a1e3e048e702" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{12}{7}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330828} {:answer/id #uuid "8e09b5f1-610e-543d-a7c6-08d3a9552d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331210} {:answer/id #uuid "d50b0165-c443-5756-9dab-ed664f6ac4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{3}{27}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331837} {:answer/id #uuid "f64e18c1-7361-5c91-a11c-cf60c4d2c4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f64e18c1-7361-5c91-a11c-cf60c4d2c4cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332111} :answer-field/id #uuid "28655b4d-4ed3-526e-a0ba-2d5db4da6116" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee166994-93a7-5ed5-b2a0-5e87a17a6753" :question/math-academy-id "q-50682" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}$ about $x=0$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+x}}=(1+x)^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x)^{-3/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{3}{4}(1+x)^{-5/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2})x+(\\frac{3}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{8}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1050764e-7fd3-504b-b930-0b70731d5ce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{12}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330093} {:answer/id #uuid "20987c66-fd52-5b6a-8710-65a1fdbe2f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)+\\frac{1}{81}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330230} {:answer/id #uuid "a428bfb1-2d01-5cf5-b609-dcaaca7deb17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{32}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331421} {:answer/id #uuid "abad7af0-f57a-57c0-a169-4b768d06243f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-4)+\\frac{1}{16}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} {:answer/id #uuid "c734d130-e798-5c19-9d87-eacd2011be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c734d130-e798-5c19-9d87-eacd2011be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331712} :answer-field/id #uuid "13b85753-ad9b-50e0-a39b-df6c9c6048cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce167e8c-c6fa-5855-bad7-6c074cc092ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-50683" :question/problem "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=4$ is" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4,$ we plug $a=4$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(4),$ ${f}^{′}(4),$ and ${f}^{″}(4):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} & ⟹ &  & f(4) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(4),$ ${f}^{′}(4),$ and ${f}^{″}(4)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-(\\frac{1}{32})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x-4)^{2} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{4}(x-4)-\\frac{1}{64}(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "624b7117-00f8-551a-809e-5d1c3ff1debc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467692} {:answer/id #uuid "a1161ac7-f2a2-58a6-8a07-6b82fbac85a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1161ac7-f2a2-58a6-8a07-6b82fbac85a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467693} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b52ba51-dd6a-5109-ad4a-86c7ab07785f" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468567} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a95e3e6-c21e-5dba-88b4-2aa448e29ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467694} {:answer/id #uuid "c80a45eb-af40-56af-be7f-df024e54b91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a95e3e6-c21e-5dba-88b4-2aa448e29ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467694} :answer-field/id #uuid "640dfa81-f2e1-5fca-a3e6-abf23e497d33" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468568} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52924de1-0701-5aac-9b3a-e34d33354697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467696} {:answer/id #uuid "dde7684e-a4ef-50e4-a409-ed9aeefc8f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52924de1-0701-5aac-9b3a-e34d33354697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467696} :answer-field/id #uuid "20a21870-3114-52a9-bdfe-348f688b5b7a" :answer-field/key "${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" :question/math-academy-id "q-50971" :question/problem "Which of the following series converge?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$ converges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d794c3d-0f93-5e9b-859b-c340a6282610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330206} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2fc078-2b62-5d65-868e-79b45fc7931e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330500} {:answer/id #uuid "43112ebc-3e8a-5866-aa5d-6e50c43cc18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330551} {:answer/id #uuid "d79174d3-f14b-59a5-9d4a-8261c63a8d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331855} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6ec74e-8319-5846-b8a1-18ee324765f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d79174d3-f14b-59a5-9d4a-8261c63a8d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331855} :answer-field/id #uuid "711e8728-f052-51b4-a0b9-2a8ed9eb7bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54f3f214-d6dd-5486-9655-fb3920d2f817" :question/math-academy-id "q-51109" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡u}{2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "590d6352-1cb2-5f07-b337-1a5ebbae468c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330748} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5d39fd-d361-5948-ac25-d41727644ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331468} {:answer/id #uuid "b88545d6-ff7b-56de-916f-9ba100472dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6a7743-731d-57fb-8d43-9d7442d6b434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332006} {:answer/id #uuid "fa786860-e6f1-53ee-9dfb-09fcd349a209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b88545d6-ff7b-56de-916f-9ba100472dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331581} :answer-field/id #uuid "18da3dd1-3b0e-5e20-8435-771e2e44e2a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "132c7e25-b8d1-5b60-bc80-45357d89a5d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51126" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we use the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x={cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}({cos}^{2}⁡2x-{sin}^{2}⁡2x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /24}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{4}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /24} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{24})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332856 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f13677-df0c-5227-8bba-34d6b80ddae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330000} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9e27b7-7095-5a8b-9710-921e741f0993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330191} {:answer/id #uuid "61a315c1-7847-5d25-940f-454f224005a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330820} {:answer/id #uuid "625fb442-1570-580f-a661-15a8d06659a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330832} {:answer/id #uuid "96d16d6e-76a3-5966-8193-677ccf5c270b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96d16d6e-76a3-5966-8193-677ccf5c270b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331289} :answer-field/id #uuid "c619486b-e757-52b4-910a-62ef15d35a5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8957bff-2acf-5eaf-99ad-a3f22d63d361" :question/math-academy-id "q-51143" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+31}{(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-2}+\\frac{B}{(x-2)^{2}}+\\frac{C}{2x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$, $B$, and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A\\cdot B\\cdot C.$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x-2)^{2}(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+31 & =A(x-2)(2x+1)+B(2x+1)+C(x-2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+31 & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+B(2[-\\frac{1}{2}]+1)+C{(-\\frac{1}{2}-2)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+31 & =A(-\\frac{5}{2})(-1+1)+B(-1+1)+C(\\frac{25}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{125}{4} & =\\frac{25C}{4} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=2$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2}+31 & =A(2-2)(2\\cdot 2+1)+B(2\\cdot 2+1)+C(2-2)^{2} \\\\ 35 & =5B \\\\ B & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, letting $x=0$ and using our values for $B$ and $C$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+31 & =A(0-2)(2\\cdot 0+1)+7(2\\cdot 0+1)+5(0-2)^{2} \\\\ 31 & =-2A+7+20 \\\\ 31 & =-2A+27 \\\\ 2A & =-4 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nHence,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot B\\cdot C=(-2)\\cdot 7\\cdot 5=-70.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332861 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41568bc8-66e5-5b92-91c4-58bf4fc1454e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330528} {:answer/id #uuid "54db32e5-e7f2-5e11-aad2-ade2bfba19a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330715} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d1d05c-6a03-5691-a924-a5b82477104f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331410} {:answer/id #uuid "bd415b93-19f9-5d77-9f40-160be3444725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{\\pi /6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331620} {:answer/id #uuid "ef74e128-de92-54ef-9418-a05286dd0154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54db32e5-e7f2-5e11-aad2-ade2bfba19a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330715} :answer-field/id #uuid "575b490b-6032-578f-8fda-8c94315455f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0690f2a6-3e10-52f0-a935-4f8552e1024c" :question/math-academy-id "q-51201" :question/problem "![](images/80/8076c5991e4c66bb505fa1800ce3d78fab0fc115560ef59efb72e6776976c977.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=5{e}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=5{e}^{\\theta },$ wehave\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(5{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(5{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{25{e}^{2\\theta }+25{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{50{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /6}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332870 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0374a7-aac2-5b65-b63b-48b09372f6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\pi ,\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330064} {:answer/id #uuid "125bdfb5-0185-5ef4-912f-c54813535009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330107} {:answer/id #uuid "4e619186-79a8-531a-acbe-8d7c8870dff8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,\\,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330663} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe43ae-4302-50f4-aad8-8fc5fdfbea38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330786} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c9277-e964-5ba1-be5a-ed1dae3ec9dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨1,\\,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dbe43ae-4302-50f4-aad8-8fc5fdfbea38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330786} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3c4445-717d-550d-bf87-b5e255d63938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6043983c-7a93-58b2-8d5d-03f2c93edce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-51321" :question/problem "The position vector $r$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity vector $v$ at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1),1+(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057e127d-c807-591d-8ab4-95e22bc9faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330004} {:answer/id #uuid "14f48743-4662-5bb5-b53a-426fce87fd5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330117} {:answer/id #uuid "24a5e5b3-07af-51e2-9216-21fb858e7133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330262} {:answer/id #uuid "bb79a6c3-0dd4-5153-a975-22a9b4c3f6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331602} {:answer/id #uuid "fe8e9006-4b8f-5722-ab7b-2a091cf005e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057e127d-c807-591d-8ab4-95e22bc9faaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330004} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e03d404-348d-518f-9017-8816be664314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4bf41725-c484-5d60-bf51-85bf60b6feec" :question/math-academy-id "q-51336" :question/problem "If $x=\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(3,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{8}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{-1}+8{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-1})+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-{y}^{-2})+8\\cdot (-2{y}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2{y}^{-2}-16{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2y-16}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y+16}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2y+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,2),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,2)} & =-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{2(2)+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a920f7-9f20-5208-9e1d-0c6aab2b18b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330318} {:answer/id #uuid "3492e549-cc13-5db4-85c9-4d751fad6572" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330414} {:answer/id #uuid "41cbb68b-da18-52b9-a043-a839e1494c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330534} {:answer/id #uuid "b67595f7-0643-551e-8f5d-d302ee0b8c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331561} {:answer/id #uuid "f249507b-43e5-55ec-a0bf-fb51853c2209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b67595f7-0643-551e-8f5d-d302ee0b8c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331561} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e34bfe-fc8c-5d9f-be31-ce6a63c9c697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18252347-e352-523e-8b93-4f58594c62c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51354" :question/problem "The velocity $v$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position $(0,0)$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ calculate the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore, the position vector is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1⟩.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a795cb-9f0e-5939-900e-adafa303f8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330103} {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} {:answer/id #uuid "72076787-7552-58b8-8ad4-00b516cbaf7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "98ee28e9-1737-5e3b-81c5-05141104f588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(6x-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331315} {:answer/id #uuid "f630c36b-13b8-5df3-93c0-7e5b92d1a0cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586c59c4-4661-5f6d-8c14-a8719591fc01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330738} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d388fcd-2c84-5c86-86ee-03feb01e614c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38a732d9-8b0e-58f3-ab88-f23eff634c61" :question/math-academy-id "q-52537" :question/problem "Rewrite $6{x}^{2}-2x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $6$. So, we take out a factor of $6$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$: \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}x+{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}x) & =6({(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{36} \\\\  & =6{(x-\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332898 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19984c41-2e9b-55a7-a17c-f6a14e8face7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330171} {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} {:answer/id #uuid "90067c4f-ae37-5430-8ae1-5960ae9a9184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331230} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cc8021-894c-56c3-9e32-003542b0ae5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331811} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0b2fc2-d00a-5cb2-8558-20bf23e0f5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a19d3b2-f62f-5c65-8cbc-bf66ae76b565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330755} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8576bd1-557c-5066-8b28-c3980d1e6e26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "178ea495-c20f-5cdc-a1d6-8971b012cd62" :question/math-academy-id "q-53745" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ for the curve ${x}^{2}y=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the ${x}^{2}y$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-1) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)+y\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,-1)$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{2y}{x})}_{(1,\\,-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab9861f-bd24-5e05-a3df-1a270b940998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330329} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4f63f-b107-58fc-9a69-479fc30539ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331129} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a74c7f-fa05-586c-91b2-b13729730ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331534} {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} {:answer/id #uuid "f3011a9e-cdfd-5574-baad-72ca2f4d675d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c67f73c6-f999-5750-bbec-ae12147c6bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331708} :answer-field/id #uuid "8801c2b3-5109-5311-a205-3b2d57c10695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c73f48a1-5f73-5fca-87a5-5adf2b36d0e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-53747" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ for the curve $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope at a point, we use implicit differentiation to calculate the derivative of the curve, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\nNote that to differentiate the $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)$ term, we will need to use the product rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) & =0 \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)}{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the tangent at the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is then given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)}{x})}_{(2,\\pi /4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45b557f6-3edb-5181-83a9-d501c8f38e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330576} {:answer/id #uuid "4615b002-6927-5b19-8fec-0a981bf28557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330580} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} {:answer/id #uuid "ab557b79-efa8-5221-881f-bc531ab4bbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,14)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331467} {:answer/id #uuid "eae5ddb8-9bf5-5595-a494-19c67808b88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea9c192-2487-5f0c-88d6-bba4bc8d15c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331372} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c83ec8-c729-560d-934b-2c4ab7bca24f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b7bca4e-70fe-57a8-8703-2bc1be5d133a" :question/math-academy-id "q-53750" :question/problem "![](images/fa/faa8a8e0521972fa860d474ac079405b6930df6c47fda05808fdf242d6d6922b.png)\n\nThe equation $xy=5$ represents a hyperbola. The tangent line at the point $(1,5)$ on the hyperbola intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the tangent line at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xy) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =0 \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$  is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{y}{x})}_{(1,\\,5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{1} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write the equation of the tangent at the point $(1,\\,5)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-5) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ y & =-5(x-1)+5 \\\\ y & =-5x+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this tangent line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-5\\cdot 0+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,10).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e24427-f27b-58e1-a535-5687071b81fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330232} {:answer/id #uuid "40cb1e6a-90d3-51f1-81e1-ce30d72daf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330525} {:answer/id #uuid "41f3c393-cb41-5d4c-979b-630ddf43e5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330536} {:answer/id #uuid "543a9b07-5657-5aae-9f12-b5ea7fabce5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330710} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc8c4cd-4e0f-51c5-9d3f-13e2c083c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cb1e6a-90d3-51f1-81e1-ce30d72daf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330525} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c19b83-976d-5b73-a6d8-c0fb19b2c51f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b77b6e3d-1b8e-5eff-92f7-2ee055617638" :question/math-academy-id "q-54151" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}.$ Given  that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{t}^{2}+1)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{2t} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c373fbd-d448-580e-9e69-f5e2321837d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330642} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd654ed-9ef2-5836-a969-bbd6e5d1d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330770} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b56339-361d-5fc3-8351-4ee1bb4246cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331455} {:answer/id #uuid "d572db6e-67b4-5b17-acd7-f4c6f80401ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331843} {:answer/id #uuid "e5923c5b-2608-5670-87a4-c541cf4a47ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd654ed-9ef2-5836-a969-bbd6e5d1d37c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330770} :answer-field/id #uuid "2584732a-3f32-502d-9705-dd3a1eae011e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdfc853f-2868-50fe-a496-48e0f43e6760" :question/math-academy-id "q-54152" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={t}^{2},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}})=-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2})}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{(-\\frac{3}{{t}^{4}})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a70d6f-694d-5a0e-b313-07a62fe05242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330303} {:answer/id #uuid "66f93660-94cf-53f8-a13d-3b62b6ee3cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330872} {:answer/id #uuid "bb8faefc-b8a6-5649-818c-4951e5b1b3a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331604} {:answer/id #uuid "ce143c00-0b59-5fbb-b15d-50673b390da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331778} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a621c5-d433-5e88-a939-a5dbb1568466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27a70d6f-694d-5a0e-b313-07a62fe05242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330303} :answer-field/id #uuid "9350cec8-b435-563e-8a55-cf4516cb7166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e17b1aa-cc22-5c50-9e89-7364972255b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-54154" :question/problem "A curve has parametric equations $x=2{e}^{t},\\,y={e}^{2t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}={e}^{t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{t})=2{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{t})}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{2{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ is constant, we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=3}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332908 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03368465-f6c9-5432-a81e-079cce79d6ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329990} {:answer/id #uuid "545d815d-38c1-5363-9613-df1e0d53653e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330711} {:answer/id #uuid "99dee500-541d-56a0-86db-4d99f94554ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331330} {:answer/id #uuid "be9550e1-d095-5cab-9af4-5f9a8dc1a9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331636} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9922b-dd11-5b47-824c-a9e3371ac401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9550e1-d095-5cab-9af4-5f9a8dc1a9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331636} :answer-field/id #uuid "327842f2-2e8e-544e-ad91-985c3cdb707f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40be294f-6a5a-5ee0-a717-dcefdad171be" :question/math-academy-id "q-54156" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{5}+2{t}^{3}$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{5{t}^{4}+6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}=5{t}^{2}+6.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(5{t}^{2}+6)}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0720f878-f9e6-57b7-a9ad-2cafdbf9fd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{csc}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330012} {:answer/id #uuid "60210d84-bcdd-576f-90af-4f4b4412e584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330802} {:answer/id #uuid "6697d4e8-d25b-57a8-8cd4-582cc3f62c33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡t}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330870} {:answer/id #uuid "9a34ba3f-6e5c-57fc-b2c0-b71cbd4bde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331333} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d5e7fb-cd5f-5e32-b78c-1ece3aded26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡t}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a34ba3f-6e5c-57fc-b2c0-b71cbd4bde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331333} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb631b10-44cd-5de1-b74f-0fe092fa9f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e1d829e6-cbc8-5156-8b9c-b8c7a64d3319" :question/math-academy-id "q-54157" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3{csc}^{2}⁡t}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332910 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07a75984-9452-5895-86f4-451320d425a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330016} {:answer/id #uuid "69f02701-58cc-567e-b606-fd7488ea03f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330899} {:answer/id #uuid "8013238c-2bdb-5df1-a2d2-be71f3393693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331081} {:answer/id #uuid "8348c8ca-0586-58c1-bba7-ccb3d6b6864f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331117} {:answer/id #uuid "d7af10db-9ba9-5aba-8e43-22c26fa0fecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69f02701-58cc-567e-b606-fd7488ea03f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330899} :answer-field/id #uuid "07a07624-21c5-565b-98f3-3c972e090388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c80a94c-01f7-551a-aa91-f7d42ace55c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-54158" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{4t}.$ Given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{e}^{2t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{e}^{2t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e1d272-c680-5ddf-bf8f-1cf5ef2c8a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330240} {:answer/id #uuid "495f8d96-74b3-5132-b9f7-37586da7b0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330608} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f44f11-cc85-5e21-9914-0e5cbdee3c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331428} {:answer/id #uuid "af8e591f-1645-5e67-bc31-3e18c93ba992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331500} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ccc595-7b4f-5947-85c7-b60389376f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3ccc595-7b4f-5947-85c7-b60389376f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331539} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb99eac2-3ea2-532a-af56-82af55d239a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af7b2808-40f2-53f1-87fb-a46df1ec93dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-54161" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-1,\\,y=4{t}^{5}+1$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=20{t}^{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{20{t}^{4}}{2t}=10{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(10{t}^{3})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{30{t}^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =15t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3ca98b-b4e1-583e-9b95-7fb21be04c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330342} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6d19d9-5642-56a6-96d4-87c33e2c53d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17